    (
        'Python Programming Fundamentals',
        'python-fundamentals',
        'Environment setup, syntax, dynamic typing, bitwise operators, logic, loops, functions, collections, and reference mutability',
        '# Python Programming Fundamentals\n\n## Beginner: Environment Setup, Syntax & Dynamic Typing\nPython is an interpreted, dynamically typed language widely used for AI, data science, web backends, and automation.\n- Verify install in terminal: `python --version`\n- Key terminal commands: `pwd`, `ls`/`dir`, `cd`, `mkdir`.\n- Primitive types: `str`, `int`, `float`, `bool`. Python infers variable types automatically at runtime.\n\n### Operators & Bitwise Shift Operations\n- Arithmetic: `+ - * / % // **`\n- Logical: `and or not`\n- Bitwise (Integers only): `<<` (left shift, multiplies by 2^n), `>>` (right shift, floor divides by 2^n), `&` (AND), `|` (OR), `^` (XOR).\n\n## Intermediate: Control Flow & Data Structures\n- Block indentation: Mandatory 4-space indentation.\n- Iteration: `for i in range(start, stop, step)` or sequence iteration; `while condition:` for dynamic loops. `break` exits, `continue` skips.\n- Functions: Defined with `def fn(params):`. Parameters pass inputs, `return` yields values, `pass` is a syntactic placeholder.\n- Collections: Lists (mutable, ordered), Strings (immutable Unicode), Dictionaries (keyed key-value map, insertion ordered, `get(key, default)` avoids KeyError).\n\n## Advanced & Experienced Engineer: Production Patterns & Memory\n- Reference model: Immutable types (int, str, tuple) create new objects on edit; mutable types (list, dict, set) pass by reference.\n- Pitfall: Default mutable argument (`def fn(arr=[])`). Correct pattern uses `def fn(arr=None): if arr is None: arr = []`.\n- High performance dict/list comprehensions for data processing.',
    ),
